  "sections": {
    "BASICS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Formation of a thin soft tissue membrane or a fibrous band of tissue at the choanae or anywhere above the soft palate, resulting in the occlusion of the caudal nasopharyngeal openings or narrowing of the orifice from a 1+ cm oval opening to a 1- to 2-mm opening or less.",
              "pdf_page": 1042,
              "printed_page": 960,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Inflammation secondary to chronic rhinitis, or from regurgitation or vomiting of material into the nasopharynx, particularly after anesthesia, should be considered as possible causes. Might also occur secondary to lodging of a foreign body in the choana.",
              "pdf_page": 1042,
              "printed_page": 960,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Congenital narrowing or dysgenesis of the region can also be encountered; thickened palatopharyngeal muscles have been reported as a cause of nasopharyngeal stenosis in the dachshund.",
              "pdf_page": 1042,
              "printed_page": 960,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
